##
   A pointer receiver is the form that actually reaches the caller's storage.

   Three details this pins down:
   - `mut` belongs on the *pointee* (`ptr<mut T>`), not the pointer. Dereferencing
     does not strip a `mut` qualifier, so `mut ptr<T>` is not dereferenceable
     at all.
   - The receiver must *be* a pointer: the call is `p.set(99)`, not
     `c.set(99)`. Passing the struct where a pointer is expected is rejected,
     because the method's first parameter is checked like any other argument.
   - Writing through it needs `marked`, because `^` is a raw dereference.

   The receiver binding itself is still not reassignable -- `self` here is a
   `ptr` value, not a `mut ptr` one -- which is exactly the per-depth rule the
   const/mut validator applies to ordinary bindings.

   Method *lookup* resolves through the pointer to find `Counter_set`, but the
   pointer itself is what gets passed, and the body still needs an explicit `^`
   to touch a field. Nothing is dereferenced implicitly.
##
struct Counter { n: i32 }

impl Counter {
  func set(self: ptr<mut Counter>, v: i32): i32 {
    var wrote = marked { ^self.n = v; 0i32 };
    return wrote;
  }
  func read(self: ptr<Counter>): i32 {
    var got = marked { ^self.n; 0i32 };
    return got;
  }
}

func mutate_caller(): i32 {
  var c: mut Counter = .Counter{.n = 5i32};
  var p: ptr<mut Counter> = @c;
  p.set(99i32);
  return c.n;
}
